1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,458
For two hundred twenty years, Japan locked
its doors.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,370
Sneak out, and the punishment was death.

3
00:00:06,390 --> 00:00:08,574
Come back, and it was death again.

4
00:00:09,342 --> 00:00:12,098
Then four black ships arrived, and within

5
00:00:12,118 --> 00:00:15,503
twenty-three years, the samurai lost their
swords.

6
00:00:16,368 --> 00:00:19,459
This is Japan, the Land of the Rising Sun.

7
00:00:19,886 --> 00:00:22,234
It has fourteen thousand one hundred
twenty-five

8
00:00:22,254 --> 00:00:25,116
islands, double what it counted until
twenty twenty-three.

9
00:00:25,674 --> 00:00:28,295
Here is how the doors closed, how they
burst

10
00:00:28,315 --> 00:00:31,016
open, and which one is still waiting for
you.

11
00:00:31,886 --> 00:00:32,948
So why lock them?

12
00:00:32,968 --> 00:00:37,868
Historians say the shogun wanted to cut
off Spanish and Portuguese influence.

13
00:00:38,406 --> 00:00:41,271
From sixteen thirty-three to sixteen
thirty-nine,

14
00:00:41,291 --> 00:00:44,177
edicts banned Japanese ships from sailing
abroad.

15
00:00:44,635 --> 00:00:46,695
But Japan was never fully sealed.

16
00:00:46,715 --> 00:00:49,657
On Dejima, a fan-shaped island just one
hundred

17
00:00:49,677 --> 00:00:52,954
twenty meters long, the Dutch were allowed
to trade.

18
00:00:53,448 --> 00:00:55,957
Through it flowed Dutch books and
medicine.

19
00:00:55,977 --> 00:00:58,506
Japanese scholars called it Dutch
learning.

20
00:00:59,008 --> 00:01:03,004
Two doctors spent three years translating
a single Dutch anatomy book.

21
00:01:03,716 --> 00:01:06,985
Then, on July eighth, eighteen
fifty-three, four

22
00:01:07,005 --> 00:01:10,226
warships entered the bay at Edo, today's
Tokyo.

23
00:01:10,704 --> 00:01:12,391
No sails. Only black smoke.

24
00:01:12,411 --> 00:01:15,892
People thought they were giant dragons
puffing smoke.

25
00:01:16,439 --> 00:01:19,141
Commodore Perry handed over a letter,
promised to

26
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:21,494
return, and came back with a bigger fleet.

27
00:01:22,274 --> 00:01:25,497
In March eighteen fifty-four, Japan signed
the Treaty

28
00:01:25,517 --> 00:01:28,516
of Kanagawa, opening two ports to American
ships.

29
00:01:28,991 --> 00:01:33,365
Signed under threat of force, it ended
about two hundred twenty years of seclusion.

30
00:01:34,044 --> 00:01:37,848
What followed was one of the fastest
national makeovers in modern history.

31
00:01:38,365 --> 00:01:41,707
In eighteen sixty-eight, samurai loyalists
seized the palace

32
00:01:41,727 --> 00:01:44,865
in Kyoto and restored a fifteen-year-old
emperor, Meiji.

33
00:01:45,445 --> 00:01:50,318
Within three years, the old feudal domains
were gone, replaced by prefectures.

34
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:53,119
In eighteen seventy-two, the first railway

35
00:01:53,139 --> 00:01:56,229
opened, twenty-nine kilometers from Tokyo
to Yokohama.

36
00:01:56,712 --> 00:01:57,978
And here is the twist.

37
00:01:57,998 --> 00:02:02,442
The leaders of this revolution were
samurai, and they abolished the samurai.

38
00:02:02,901 --> 00:02:05,482
Their stipends ended in eighteen
seventy-three.

39
00:02:05,502 --> 00:02:09,432
In eighteen seventy-six, they lost the
right to carry swords in public.

40
00:02:09,861 --> 00:02:14,554
In eighteen seventy-seven, about fifteen
thousand former samurai rebelled.

41
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:17,377
They lost, and it was the last big
uprising.

42
00:02:18,002 --> 00:02:20,482
But one door still shapes your trip.

43
00:02:20,502 --> 00:02:24,392
It is the genkan, the entrance where
outdoor shoes stop.

44
00:02:24,875 --> 00:02:28,086
Japan draws a clear line between inside
and outside.

45
00:02:28,106 --> 00:02:31,523
Never step onto the raised floor in your
outdoor shoes.

46
00:02:31,913 --> 00:02:33,015
Slippers wait there.

47
00:02:33,035 --> 00:02:35,727
Take them off again before stepping onto
tatami.

48
00:02:36,222 --> 00:02:39,505
In the bathroom, there is a second pair:
toilet slippers.

49
00:02:39,525 --> 00:02:43,872
Walk out still wearing them, and you have
made the classic tourist mistake.

50
00:02:44,344 --> 00:02:45,970
Outside, the rules continue.

51
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:48,499
Public trash cans are rare, except at
train

52
00:02:48,519 --> 00:02:51,635
stations and convenience stores, so carry
your trash.

53
00:02:51,986 --> 00:02:53,620
And do not eat while walking.

54
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,522
Sit down, or step out of the way.

55
00:02:56,111 --> 00:02:57,709
Japan locked its doors once.

56
00:02:57,729 --> 00:02:58,922
Today they open wide.

57
00:02:58,942 --> 00:03:01,195
Just know where to take off your shoes.

58
00:03:02,157 --> 00:03:04,802
That was Japan. Which country should we
visit next?

59
00:03:04,822 --> 00:03:06,101
Tell me in the comments.

